    contentEn: `<h2>Getting from Puerto de Mogán to Taurito</h2>
<p>The trip from Puerto de Mogán to Taurito covers about 18 kilometres along the winding GC-500 coastal road and takes roughly 18 minutes by private car. The route hugs the coastline through a landscape of old volcanic cliffs, offering sea views on one side and dramatic rock formations on the other, and a private transfer takes you door to door — collected from your location in Puerto de Mogán and driven directly to your accommodation in Taurito, with no waiting on a bus timetable or working out connections.</p>
<p>This route is most often booked by visitors staying in Taurito who've spent the day or evening in Puerto de Mogán, whether for its Friday market, its restaurants along the harbour, or simply a wander through the canal-side streets, and now need a straightforward way back to their hotel. It's especially useful after dark, when a fixed pickup with a driver already tracking your plans removes any uncertainty about getting back to a smaller, quieter resort area like Taurito, which doesn't have the same density of taxis passing through as the bigger resort towns nearby.</p>
<p>You can arrange your pickup point, timing, and a fixed price in advance through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>. If your day in Puerto de Mogán is part of a longer Gran Canaria itinerary that also includes airport travel or other stops along the coast, our airport transfers and <a href="/city-to-city-transfers/">city-to-city transfers</a> services can be combined with this route so everything is arranged as one coordinated plan rather than separate bookings.</p>
<h2>About Puerto de Mogán</h2>
<p>Puerto de Mogán is one of Gran Canaria's most recognisable harbour towns, built around a network of ornamental canals and small footbridges lined with whitewashed, bougainvillea-covered buildings that have earned it the nickname "Little Venice." The marina handles pleasure boats, fishing vessels, and day-trip catamarans, and the largely pedestrianised streets around the water make it a relaxed place to spend an afternoon or evening without needing a fixed plan.</p>
<h3>Market Day and Evenings by the Harbour</h3>
<p>The town's well-known market runs on Fridays, filling the streets near the harbour with stalls of crafts, clothing, and local products, and drawing shoppers from across the surrounding coast, Taurito included. Outside market hours, the harbour promenade is lined with restaurants and cafés that make it a popular dinner destination, and many visitors staying in the quieter resort of Taurito make the short trip over specifically for an evening meal before returning to their hotel.</p>
<h2>About Taurito</h2>
<p>Taurito is a smaller, more contained resort area set within a natural amphitheatre of volcanic cliffs, positioned between the larger developments of Puerto Rico and Puerto de Mogán. Its defining feature is an artificial black-sand beach set inside a partly enclosed lagoon, giving swimmers calmer water than much of the open coastline nearby, and the adjoining Lago Taurito waterpark area adds pools and water slides built around the same sheltered stretch of coast.</p>
<h3>A Calmer Return Point</h3>
<p>Compared to the bustle of Puerto de Mogán's harbour or the density of Puerto Rico, Taurito offers a noticeably quieter place to return to at the end of a day out, its resort hotels set against a striking backdrop of steep volcanic slopes rising directly behind the beach. For visitors who've chosen Taurito specifically for that calmer pace, a direct private transfer back from Puerto de Mogán fits the same logic as the choice of resort itself — a straightforward, unhurried way to end the day rather than navigating public transport after dark in an area with fewer passing taxis.</p>
<h3>Timing Your Return</h3>
<p>Because Taurito sees less through-traffic than its neighbours, arranging your return transfer in advance, rather than trying to find transport on the spot after an evening in Puerto de Mogán, is generally the more reliable approach, particularly late in the evening when options thin out considerably.</p>
<h3>Trading Canals for a Quiet Beach Day</h3>
<p>Puerto de Mogán rewards a slow wander, but it doesn't offer much in the way of open swimming or family pool space of its own — the appeal here is the canals, the market, and the marina rather than the shoreline itself. Taurito, eighteen minutes back along the coast, fills that gap neatly. Its artificial black-sand beach sits inside a partly enclosed lagoon that keeps the water calmer than most of the open coastline nearby, and the adjoining Lago Taurito complex adds a series of pools and water slides for visitors who'd rather not rely on the sea at all. For anyone based in Puerto de Mogán for the canal views and harbourside dining, a day trip out to Taurito's beach and waterpark is a straightforward way to add a proper beach day to a stay that's otherwise built around wandering rather than swimming.</p>
<h3>Booking for a Short, Simple Journey</h3>
<p>Because this is one of the shorter routes on this stretch of coast, it's easy to assume booking details matter less than they would for a longer transfer — in practice, the opposite is often true. Groups travelling together should still confirm numbers and luggage in advance, since Taurito's beach day almost always means extra bags for towels, swimwear, and pool gear that a standard car might not comfortably fit alongside everyone's seating. Confirming your exact pickup point in Puerto de Mogán, particularly if you're coming from inside the pedestrianised harbour area rather than a hotel with direct road access, also helps the driver find you quickly rather than circling the one-way streets near the canals looking for a group waiting at the wrong corner.</p>
<p>Because the drive itself takes less time than checking out of a hotel room, most of what determines a smooth trip happens before the car even arrives: a clear pickup spot, an accurate headcount, and a rough idea of how much beach and pool gear is coming along, all agreed at the time of booking rather than sorted out at the roadside.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>
<p>Groups and families should mention their size and any luggage or equipment when booking so the right vehicle is arranged ahead of time — our group event transfers service is built for larger parties travelling together. Traffic along the GC-500 between Puerto de Mogán and Taurito is generally light, though it can be busier on Friday market days, so treat the stated drive time as a reasonable estimate rather than an exact figure. See our <a href="/gran-canaria/">Gran Canaria</a> page for more on the island as a whole, and our <a href="/hourly-chauffeur/">hourly chauffeur</a> service if you'd like a driver available for a full evening out rather than a single point-to-point trip.</p>`,
